//# Core exception effect labels (#1279 alias bridge, #1344 typed kinds)
//
// ADR-0085 (docs/internal/design/exception-effect.md). Every spelling check for the core
// exception effect lives here so the migration has ONE replacement boundary.
//
// The label grammar this module recognizes:
//
//   Exception                  erased (any kind)            -- CANONICAL
//   Error                      erased (any kind)            -- accepted alias
//   Exception[IoError]         KINDED (#1344, ADR-0085 Phase 3)
//   <any of the above>::Throw  the operation label of that effect
//
// #1461 flipped the canonical erased spelling from `Error` to `Exception`.
// `Error` is still accepted on input everywhere it was, but nothing the
// checker stores or prints uses it any more. Removing it from the grammar
// entirely is the last step of that migration and needs a seed bump first.
//
// The KIND is the static type of the thrown payload. Two kinded labels with
// different kinds are different row elements: `Exception[IoError]` neither
// authorizes nor discharges `Exception[ParseError]`.
//
// **`Error` is the ERASED exception row, not `Exception[String]`.** ADR-0085's
// migration section calls `Error` a compatibility alias *of* `Exception[String]`;
// that reading cannot hold while #786 (`throw(KeyInvalid("x"))` under a plain
// `with Exception`) is live, because a String-kinded row would reject every
// suberror throw in the existing codebase. So the implementation reads the
// alias as kind-erased -- strictly WEAKER than any kinded label, compatible
// with all of them. See docs/internal/design/exception-effect.md ("What `Error` means").
//
// Runtime identity is deliberately NOT split by kind: every spelling lowers to
// the same abortive Wasm exception tag (ADR-0085 defers the per-kind tag / type
// id ABI choice). The kind lives entirely in the checker's row.

/// The bracketed KIND argument of an exception EFFECT label, or "" when the
/// label is an erased spelling (or not an exception label at all).
/// `Exception[IoError]` -> "IoError"; `Error` / `Exception` -> "".
export fn exception_effect_kind(name: String) -> String {
  // (`open` is a reserved word in vibe, hence the suffixed name.)
  let open_at = String::index_of(name, "[")
  if open_at > 0 && String::substring(name, 0, open_at) == "Exception" && String::length(name) > open_at + 2 && String::substring(name, String::length(name) - 1, String::length(name)) == "]" {
    String::substring(name, open_at + 1, String::length(name) - 1)
  } else {
    ""
  }
}

/// True for either erased spelling or a kinded `Exception[K]` of the interim
/// abortive exception effect.
export fn is_exception_effect_name(name: String) -> Bool {
  name == "Error" || name == "Exception" || String::length(exception_effect_kind(name)) > 0
}

/// Canonical checker/codegen row label. The two ERASED spellings collapse to
/// `Exception` (#1461: the one spelling the language keeps -- it is also the
/// stem of the KINDED form, so `Exception` / `Exception[IoError]` read as one
/// family where `Error` / `Exception[IoError]` did not); a KINDED label is
/// already canonical and must survive verbatim -- collapsing it would erase
/// exactly the information the row check exists to compare.
///
/// `Error` stays ACCEPTED as an input spelling (is_exception_effect_name), it
/// is simply no longer what the checker stores or any diagnostic prints.
export fn canonical_exception_effect_name(name: String) -> String {
  if name == "Error" || name == "Exception" {
    "Exception"
  } else {
    name
  }
}

/// True for any qualified operation of the exception effect.
export fn is_exception_operation_name(name: String) -> Bool {
  let split = String::index_of(name, "::")
  split > 0 && is_exception_effect_name(String::substring(name, 0, split))
}

/// True for an operation name that lowers to the shared abortive exception tag.
/// Kind-insensitive on purpose: `Exception[IoError]::Throw` and `Exception::Throw`
/// are the same runtime transfer, so every codegen/analysis caller of this
/// predicate keeps treating them as one operation.
export fn is_exception_throw_operation(name: String) -> Bool {
  let split = String::index_of(name, "::")
  split > 0 && String::substring(name, split + 2, String::length(name)) == "Throw" && is_exception_effect_name(String::substring(name, 0, split))
}

/// True for the constructor name used by an abortive exception handler arm.
/// `Throw` remains accepted for the pre-existing AST-level codegen callers.
export fn is_exception_throw_handler_name(name: String) -> Bool {
  name == "Throw" || is_exception_throw_operation(name)
}

/// True for a row label naming the exception effect, in either the bare-effect
/// (`Error`, `Exception[E]`) or the qualified-operation (`Exception::Throw`) form.
export fn is_exception_label(label: String) -> Bool {
  is_exception_effect_name(label) || is_exception_operation_name(label)
}

/// The exception kind of a row label in EITHER form -- "" for an erased label,
/// and also "" for a label this module does not recognize (callers gate on
/// is_exception_label first).
export fn exception_label_kind(label: String) -> String {
  let split = String::index_of(label, "::")
  if split > 0 {
    exception_effect_kind(String::substring(label, 0, split))
  } else {
    exception_effect_kind(label)
  }
}

/// Does a row element of kind `declared_kind` cover a requirement of kind
/// `required_kind`? "" (erased) is compatible with everything in BOTH
/// directions:
///
/// - declared erased: `with Exception` keeps authorizing every throw, which is
///   what makes this whole change purely additive for existing code.
/// - required erased: a requirement of unknown kind. This predicate still
///   calls that compatible (it is label EQUALITY, used for function-type
///   assignability). Row AUTHORIZATION is stricter: a row that declares only
///   kinds does not cover an erased requirement (#2964, #3015 --
///   `declared_exception_label_covers`, checker_effects.vibe).
export fn exception_kinds_compatible(declared_kind: String, required_kind: String) -> Bool {
  String::length(declared_kind) == 0 || String::length(required_kind) == 0 || declared_kind == required_kind || exception_kind_resolved(declared_kind) == exception_kind_resolved(required_kind)
}

/// The kinds the checker has resolved while validating rows and arms in the
/// module being checked: (written spelling without spaces, resolved type
/// spelling). A kind is a string in the row, but it names a TYPE, and two
/// spellings of one type (`Es[IoErr]` with `type Es[T] = Array[T]`, and
/// `Array[IoErr]`) must compare equal -- in the effect pass, and in
/// function-type assignability, which both compare through this module.
let exception_kind_resolution_cell: Array[(String, String)] = Array::slice([
  ("", "")
], 0, 0)

fn exception_kind_spaceless(kind: String) -> String {
  let parts = String::split(kind, " ")
  let mut acc = ""
  let mut i = 0
  while i < Array::length(parts) {
    acc = String::concat(acc, Array::get(parts, i))
    i = i + 1
  }
  acc
}

/// Record that the kind written `written` resolves to the type `resolved`.
export fn exception_kind_resolution_record(written: String, resolved: String) -> Unit {
  let w = exception_kind_spaceless(written)
  if String::length(resolved) == 0 || w == exception_kind_spaceless(resolved) || String::length(exception_kind_resolution_spelling(w)) > 0 {
    ()
  } else {
    Array::push(exception_kind_resolution_cell, (w, resolved))
  }
}

/// Forget every recorded resolution (a new module's check starts).
export fn exception_kind_resolutions_clear() -> Unit {
  Array::truncate(exception_kind_resolution_cell, 0)
}

/// The recorded resolution of `kind`, as the type is spelled, or "".
fn exception_kind_resolution_spelling(kind: String) -> String {
  let k = exception_kind_spaceless(kind)
  let mut found = ""
  let mut i = 0
  while i < Array::length(exception_kind_resolution_cell) && String::length(found) == 0 {
    let (w, r) = Array::get(exception_kind_resolution_cell, i)
    if w == k {
      found = r
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  found
}

/// `kind` in the form kinds are compared in: its recorded resolution when
/// there is one, whitespace dropped.
export fn exception_kind_resolved(kind: String) -> String {
  let r = exception_kind_resolution_spelling(kind)
  if String::length(r) > 0 {
    exception_kind_spaceless(r)
  } else {
    exception_kind_spaceless(kind)
  }
}

/// `row` with every `Exception[K]` whose kind has a recorded resolution
/// rewritten to it. A function's PUBLISHED row goes through this, so a module
/// importing it compares the resolved type even when it cannot see the alias
/// the row was written with.
export fn exception_row_resolved(row: String) -> String {
  if Array::length(exception_kind_resolution_cell) == 0 {
    row
  } else {
    let n = String::length(row)
    let mut out = ""
    let mut i = 0
    while i < n {
      let at = String::index_of(String::substring(row, i, n), "Exception[")
      if at < 0 {
        out = String::concat(out, String::substring(row, i, n))
        i = n
      } else {
        let kind_start = i + at + 10
        out = String::concat(out, String::substring(row, i, kind_start))
        // The matching `]` of this bracket.
        let mut depth = 1
        let mut j = kind_start
        while j < n && depth > 0 {
          let c = String::char_code_at(row, j)
          if c == 91 {
            depth = depth + 1
          } else if c == 93 {
            depth = depth - 1
          }
          j = j + 1
        }
        if depth == 0 {
          let kind = String::substring(row, kind_start, j - 1)
          // A spelling with a comma (a tuple alias's `(Int, Int)`, a
          // two-argument type) is not published: rows are split on commas
          // (Codex on #3021), so the kind keeps the spelling it was written
          // with -- still compared through the resolution where it is known.
          let r0 = exception_kind_resolution_spelling(kind)
          let r = if String::contains(r0, ",") {
            ""
          } else {
            r0
          }
          out = String::concat(out, String::concat(if String::length(r) > 0 {
            r
          } else {
            kind
          }, "]"))
          i = j
        } else {
          out = String::concat(out, String::substring(row, kind_start, n))
          i = n
        }
      }
    }
    out
  }
}

/// Alias- and kind-aware equality for effect-row labels and qualified Throw
/// operations. Two exception labels match when their kinds are compatible.
export fn exception_effect_labels_equal(left: String, right: String) -> Bool {
  if left == right {
    true
  } else if is_exception_label(left) && is_exception_label(right) {
    exception_kinds_compatible(exception_label_kind(left), exception_label_kind(right))
  } else {
    false
  }
}

/// The row label a `throw` of payload kind `kind` requires.
///
/// An empty (unresolved) kind produces the erased `Exception::Throw` spelling
/// (#1461 -- `Exception::Throw` before the canonical flip), which is what the
/// pervasive un-annotated throw sites report. A resolved kind produces the
/// BARE effect label `Exception[K]`
/// rather than an operation label: ADR-0071's operation granularity exists so
/// a fix-it grants one operation instead of a whole effect, and `Exception[E]`
/// declares exactly one operation, so there is nothing to narrow. It also has
/// to be a spelling the user can actually paste into a row -- the row grammar
/// (collect_row_item_targs, parser_base.vibe) accepts `Eff::Op` and `Eff[T]`
/// but not `Eff[T]::Op`, so a `Exception[IoError]::Throw` hint would name a
/// label that does not parse.
export fn exception_throw_label_for_kind(kind: String) -> String {
  if String::length(kind) == 0 {
    "Exception::Throw"
  } else {
    String::concat("Exception[", String::concat(kind, "]"))
  }
}
